        </div>

        <p>Collectors respond to artistic Western photography because it offers interpretation rather than record—vision rather than inventory. The medium delivers that vision through gallery-grade printing, archival materials, and the assurance that every image represents considered judgment, not a lucky snapshot or automated aesthetic.</p>

        <p>At K4 Studios, each print is produced on museum-quality substrates with pigment inks rated for generations of lightfastness. The artistic intent that shaped the capture carries through to the final presentation—because craft doesn't stop at the shutter.</p>

        <!-- SECTION 5: What It Is Not -->
        <div class="section-heading-with-line">
          <h2>What Artistic Western Photography Is Not</h2>
        </div>

        <p>To understand what qualifies, it helps to clarify what does not:</p>

        <ul style="list-style:disc; padding-left:1.5rem; margin:1rem 0 1.5rem;">
          <li style="margin-bottom:0.75rem;"><strong>Tourist snapshots.</strong> Imagery optimized for mass appeal, nostalgia, or souvenir value—overly saturated sunsets, clichéd cowboy poses, generic landscapes sold in gift shops.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom:0.75rem;"><strong>Documentary-only work.</strong> While documentary photography can be artistically significant, its primary function is record. Artistic photography prioritizes vision and interpretation.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom:0.75rem;"><strong>Filter-first editing.</strong> Reliance on presets or HDR excess without compositional purpose. Artistic work shapes the image intentionally—from capture through final construction.</li>
          <li style="margin-bottom:0.75rem;"><strong>Genre clichés.</strong> Wagon wheels, weathered fences, boots and spurs used as shorthand rather than compositional elements with narrative intent.</li>
        </ul>

        <p>The line is intent. If the work exists primarily to sell quickly, appeal broadly, or reinforce expectation, it is not artistic Western photography. Artistic work exists to be considered over time—returning meaning rather than immediate gratification.</p>

